::

## Time Complexity

:theorem:
:label: merge-complexity

The time complexity of :ref:merge-sort-def:: is $\Theta(n \log n)$ in all cases—best, average, and worst.

:proof:

At each recursion level, we perform $O(n)$ work to merge all subarrays. The recursion depth is $\log_2 n$ since we halve the array at each step. Therefore, total work is $O(n) \times O(\log n) = O(n \log n)$.

For the lower bound, any comparison-based sort requires $\Omega(n \log n)$ comparisons in the worst case, since there are $n!$ possible permutations and each comparison eliminates at most half. :qed:

::

## Comparison with Other Algorithms

:figure:
  :path: sort-comparison.html

  :caption: Empirical comparison of sorting algorithms on random arrays. Note how merge sort maintains consistent performance while quicksort varies with pivot selection.

::

The :ref:merge-complexity:: guarantees make merge sort ideal for\:

:itemize:

  :item: **External sorting** — when data doesn't fit in memory

  :item: **Linked lists** — where random access is expensive

  :item: **Stable sorting** — preserving order of equal elements

  :item: **Parallel processing** — independent subproblems merge naturally

::

## Trade-offs

Unlike in-place algorithms like quicksort, :ref:merge-sort-term:: requires $O(n)$ auxiliary space for the merge step. This space-time trade-off is often worthwhile for the guaranteed performance and stability.

::
